{"step_index":114,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-30T05:54:12Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-30T11:24:12+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-30T11:24:13+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/DELL/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/student-survival-app/app.js`\nTotal Lines: 2356\nTotal Bytes: 93009\nShowing lines 300 to 360\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n300:         gain.gain.setValueAtTime(0.25, now);\n301:         gain.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0.01, now + 0.6);\n302:         osc.start(now);\n303:         osc.stop(now + 0.6);\n304:       }\n305:     } catch (e) {\n306:       console.warn('Audio synthesis note:', e);\n307:     }\n308:   }\n309: \n310:   // ==========================================\n311:   // 3. PERSISTENT STATE MANAGEMENT\n312:   // ==========================================\n313:   const STORAGE_KEY = 'student_survival_app_data_v2';\n314: \n315:   let state = {\n316:     student: { ...DEFAULT_STUDENT },\n317:     subjects: [...DEFAULT_SUBJECTS],\n318:     assignments: [...DEFAULT_ASSIGNMENTS],\n319:     canteen: { ...DEFAULT_CANTEEN },\n320:     timetable: [...DEFAULT_TIMETABLE],\n321:     exams: [...DEFAULT_EXAMS],\n322:     activeTab: 'home',\n323:     selectedTimetableDay: 'Wednesday',\n324:     assignmentFilter: 'all',\n325:     selectedBrainStatus: 'zombie',\n326:   };\n327: \n328:   function loadState() {\n329:     try {\n330:       const raw = localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);\n331:       if (raw) {\n332:         const parsed = JSON.parse(raw);\n333:         if (parsed.student) state.student = { ...DEFAULT_STUDENT, ...parsed.student };\n334:         if (parsed.subjects && parsed.subjects.length) state.subjects = parsed.subjects;\n335:         if (parsed.assignments && parsed.assignments.length) state.assignments = parsed.assignments;\n336:         if (parsed.canteen) state.canteen = { ...DEFAULT_CANTEEN, ...parsed.canteen };\n337:         if (parsed.timetable && parsed.timetable.length) state.timetable = parsed.timetable;\n338:         if (parsed.exams && parsed.exams.length) state.exams = parsed.exams;\n339:       }\n340:     } catch (err) {\n341:       console.error('Error loading state from localStorage:', err);\n342:     }\n343:   }\n344: \n345:   function saveState() {\n346:     try {\n347:       localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify({\n348:         student: state.student,\n349:         subjects: state.subjects,\n350:         assignments: state.assignments,\n351:         canteen: state.canteen,\n352:         timetable: state.timetable,\n353:         exams: state.exams,\n354:       }));\n355:     } catch (err) {\n356:       console.error('Error saving state to localStorage:', err);\n357:     }\n358:   }\n359: \n360:   // ==========================================\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":115,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-30T05:54:13Z","thinking":"Initial state management requires adding new properties. These properties will track notification-related settings like the reminder interval, visibility of the notification bar, and whether desktop notifications are enabled, alongside cycle and alert management. Implement the logic to correctly load and save these new state variables during application initialization and shutdown respectively.\n\n\nLoading notification bar visibility and desktop notification enablement from parsed data is confirmed. The next step involves examining the computation of active alerts, notification bar rendering, and the periodic reminder execution flow. Designing the `computePendingAlerts()` function now includes analyzing pending assignments, sorting by due date, and generating alerts based on time remaining relative to the deadline.\n\n\nNow focusing on implementing the assignment and attendance alert logic. Assignments are being processed to determine urgency based on time remaining until the deadline, generating alerts with varying urgency levels. Attendance data is analyzed to identify potential shortages, producing appropriate alerts when attendance falls below a specified threshold.\n\n\nNow, the code examines attendance records, generating critical alerts when attendance falls below a dangerous threshold. A separate warning alert is issued if attendance is within a warning range, indicating the need for immediate improvement. Next, upcoming exams within a specific timeframe are processed, sorting exams by date to identify the closest upcoming exams.\n\n\nExam alerts are being generated based on proximity to the exam date, with distinct messages and urgency levels assigned depending on the days remaining. The logic now handles upcoming exams, creating alerts that direct users to either review the syllabus or prepare for the exam depending on the time left.  Now inspecting the function responsible for rendering the notification bar with its timers and content display.\n\n\nThe notification bar's visibility is controlled by a state variable;\n<truncated 1374 bytes>\nandleNotifBarAction\" navigates the user to an action tab when an alert with a specified tab is clicked. \"dismissNotifBar\" hides the bar, updates state, and displays a confirmation message. \"showNotifBarAgain\" restores the bar's visibility, updates the state, and displays a success message. \"toggleNotifCycle\" toggles auto-cycle state and updates the button text and displays an info message.\nThe auto-cycle function, if active, clears any existing intervals. It then starts a new interval to update a progress bar based on cycle duration. The interval updates progress and the progress bar width accordingly, but only when the cycle is active.\n\n\nThe auto-cycle function's internal timer increments a progress variable and updates a visual progress bar. This occurs repeatedly as long as the auto-cycle is active and stops otherwise. The timer resets the progress bar to 0 at the end of each cycle and restarts.\n\n\nDesktop notifications are generated only if the browser supports them and the user has granted permission.  If enabled, a new notification is created with the alert's message, an icon, and a badge.  If the user denies permission, a message is displayed indicating the lack of support, but in-app reminders remain active. When the notification bar is displayed, the logic now increments the alert index, ensuring the application cycles through all alerts. Upon permission, the system saves the enabled state and updates UI elements.\n\nDesktop notification permission request is initiated if the browser supports it. Upon granting permission, a desktop notification is created using the current alert's data; otherwise, an in-app message provides feedback.\n\nWhen the notification center is toggled, the reminder interval select value is populated. If deskto